00:00:06.389 --> 00:00:08.310 align:start position:0%
as someone who devotes hours to  discussing the deeper themes and  enriching undertones of anime it's  always been difficult for me to fully  evaluate a straightforward but genuine  romance or slice of life anime compared  to say a deep melancholic drama with its  subtle philosophical implications  and perhaps this is why it felt so  profound  when i would look up after every 20  minute episode of kagiasama love his war  with a beaming smile on my face  when i nearly cried laughing at  shivagani's attempts to dip his toes  into the world of hip-hop despite  everyone's pleas against it  when i felt such joy after witnessing  ishigami overcome his past trauma in one  of the most beautiful story arcs to be  written in anime when i felt like a  proud father seeing shivagane and kagia  finally submit in their long battle of  attrition and admit that they were in  love  [Music]  and perhaps it is for all of these same  reasons that everyone loves kagi osama  too  but it's a bit more than just that i  know  when i look at all these great parts of  kagiasama i see faint connections to  other great anime the show's  long-running dedication to gags most  notably this season's joke about  shiragon is rapping and the show's  commitment going so far as to include an  entire song and wrap piece to conclude  the arc reminded me a lot of the type of  humor i see in gintama  the short subdivided stories within each  episode done to respect each chapter of  course pave a path similar to the  disastrous life of psych ek capitalizing  on short bits to give the watcher a  comprehensive dive into every aspect of  our main cast's school life  the more connections like this that i  see  the more i am reminded of just how many  aspects of modern anime that kagiasama  has completely mastered in only its 37  episode run so far  but admittedly though i was always  certainly intrigued by the show it was  not until the end of season two reared  its head when i came to understand the  value of the anime i was watching of the  may i say  peak fiction  i was witnessing  once again perhaps it's because of the  way that i've grown to view anime and  how i naturally lean towards serious  shows but the part of kagiasama that  propelled it so high for me  was arguably its most serious part  the sports festival arc  and i feel like explaining this arc  alone first in this video can offer a  bit of insight into what i think makes  hagiasama special as a whole  this arc shows us ishigami's tragic  backstory amidst the backdrop of the  escapades of what should just be an  entertaining sports festival but what  more so acts as a plot device that  brings ishigami's growth as a character  it begins as his regretful decision to  join the cheer club and be surrounded by  peers the complete opposite of his  well disposition but through a series of  events it turns into a proud celebration  of ishigami's identity and confronting  the ghosts of his past  ishigami is like a lot of us i'm sure  a bit anti-social likes games with a  dash of pessimism and self-loathing to  top it all off but his story is stoic  and hard to watch frankly at some points  yet understandable  after beating up another student who was  mistreating his girlfriend to say the  least ishigami was told to write an  apology in order to return to school but  no matter what he did or how much his  teachers berated him he could not bring  himself to write something he did not  believe in  even if it means facing the scrutiny of  his classmates his teachers his parents  even kagi osama teaches us how difficult  it can be to stay true to ourselves and  remain unwavering like ishigami  but also  just how rewarding it can be  the finale to this arc was masterfully  done with shiragane and kagya saving  ishigami's lost soul and his spectacular  yet laughable admission to the girl he  saved long ago  the show rewards our faith in ishigami  for doing the right thing no matter what  punishment it brought and sticking  through to the end  and that ending is why we love him and  this storyline  or at least  why i do  and now i will pose the question what  about season three warranted kagyasama  to receive such high praise why does  everyone love it  well to be fair the first two seasons  were by no means low ranked it's also  often the case that the later seasons of  a good show are ranked higher than the  previous ones simply because a larger  percent of the viewers are the ones who  like the show enough to come back to it  but aside from those extraneous  logistics i believe the answer once  again lies in how the authors stayed  true to the characters and gave them the  perfect conclusion just like in the  sports festival arc  let's look at the defining component of  season 3 to further understand this  the cultural festival arc  the long-awaited dual confession  storyline in the cultural festival arc  was highly anticipated after all this  time  meaning that this finale was expected to  be nothing short of spectacular to begin  with  unlike the sports festival arc or any  arc that came before this one to be fair  this alone is the conclusion to  everything that's been built the main  premise of the story of kagya and  shiragane finally confessing to each  other  of course it's not the end of the series  but  that doesn't change the magnitude of the  confession that acts as a finale to the  entire high school story arc  nothing ruins a good show like a bad  conclusion  but nothing makes it better than a  magnificent one  if kagi osama tells us one thing after  it took three whole seasons of the show  before the main characters finally  admitted their love it tells us that  kagia and shirigane are unbending  neither will admit defeat but they will  also never stop trying  and without throwing this character  development out of the window the author  writes a conclusion in the dual  confession storyline where neither of  them actually confess to each other  shiragana asks if kagia will go to  stanford with him and she kisses him but  at the end of the long war between the  two it is only befitting and almost  poetic that neither of these incredibly  strong unbending characters ever  actually confess in the end  they can never admit defeat and outright  say it  we witnessed the ultimate display of  love from shiragane with every  last-ditch effort pulled out and  meticulously planned to make sure it all  went according to plan in a flourishing  finish to the season  one that stays truthful though to the  story it's built up to this point  it once again rewards fans for their  faith this time in khagia and shivagane  while also paying back to those of us  who believe that they as characters  would never be able to confess to each  other and made sure that they never had  to  through what i can only say is great  writing  following in the footsteps of and  expanding on what ishigami's story told  us in the season before kaguyasama once  again teaches us that it is important to  celebrate who you are and to never  change just because someone wants you to  it's human nature to stick to your guns  and even if you're lovesick or bullied  to be who you are  to be unwavering to be unbending  not only is it great writing but i think  that's a great reason alone for everyone  to love kagiasama  as always this has been the anime  culture corner i hope you enjoyed the  video don't forget to like and subscribe  for future in-depth show manga and  character analyses
 

